          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. DJI Power 1000 vs 1000 V2: Buy the V2 Unless the Gap Is Big
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common DJI question is whether the V2 is worth more than the original Power 1000. Both have the same 1,024Wh capacity, so they run a given load for the same time. The differences are in power, charging and backup behaviour.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Output: 2,600W vs 2,200W</h3>

          <p>
            The V2's listing gives 2,600W of stable output against 2,200W for the original. Both handle a refrigerator, a microwave or a circular saw. The extra 400W matters when you run two big loads at once, such as a kettle and a toaster.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Charging: 37 Minutes to 80 Percent vs 70 Minutes to Full</h3>

          <p>
            DJI lists the V2 at 0 to 80 percent in 37 minutes and the original at a full recharge in 70 minutes. Both are quick by power station standards. The V2's edge is most useful just before a storm, when you want a nearly full battery fast.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">UPS Switchover</h3>

          <p>
            The V2 and Power 2000 listings give a 10 ms switch time and describe it as twice as fast as the Power 1000. If you plan to protect a desktop computer, a NAS or network gear, that faster switch makes the V2 the safer choice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ports and Quietness</h3>

          <p>
            The V2 listing highlights dual 140W USB-C ports, enough to fast charge two big laptops; the original's listing does not give its USB-C rating, so check DJI's spec sheet if that matters to you. The original is listed as slightly quieter while recharging, 23 dB against 26 dB. If the price gap is small, buy the V2; if the original is much cheaper and you do not need UPS use for computers, it is still a good station.
          </p>
        </div>

