        <div id="size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which DJI Power Station Size Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with what you will plug in, not with the biggest number you can afford. Add the watts of the devices you will run at the same time, then estimate how many hours you need them. Our guide to <a href="/what-size-portable-power-station-do-i-need/">what size portable power station you need</a> walks through the math in detail. As a rough planning figure, assume about 85 percent of the rated watt-hours is usable after inverter losses.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Devices, Drones and Day Trips: Power 500</h3>

          <p>
            Phones, laptops, cameras, lights and drone batteries rarely add up to more than a few hundred watts. The Power 500's 512Wh covers a weekend of that, and its 1,000W limit is not a problem for those loads.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Camping, Vans and Short Outages: Power 1000 V2</h3>

          <p>
            A 1kWh unit runs a 120W-average refrigerator for roughly seven hours, a 40W CPAP for about 20, or a full evening of camp lighting, a projector and coffee in the morning. Its 2,600W output handles appliances the 500 cannot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Multi-Day Outages: Power 2000 Plus Expansion</h3>

          <p>
            For a fridge, router, lights and a furnace blower through a long outage, start with the Power 2000 and add expansion batteries as needed. One expansion battery roughly doubles its capacity. For a broader look at backup options, see our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">best portable power stations for home backup</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="home-backup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Using a DJI Power Station for Home Backup
          </h2>

          <p>
            DJI markets its larger units for home use, and the specs support it, with some limits worth stating plainly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plug Appliances In Directly</h3>

          <p>
            The simple, safe way to use any DJI Power station in an outage is to plug appliances into its outlets, or use a heavy-duty extension cord rated for the load. Never plug a power station into a wall outlet to feed power back into your home's wiring. Backfeeding can injure utility workers and damage the unit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Connecting to Home Circuits</h3>

          <p>
            If you want to power hardwired circuits such as a furnace or well pump, have a licensed electrician install a transfer switch or approved inlet that suits the station, and follow DJI's instructions and your local electrical code. The DJI listings we read do not describe 240V output, so assume 120V loads only.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">UPS Mode for Electronics</h3>

          <p>
            The Power 1000 V2 and Power 2000 list a 10 ms UPS switchover. Leave the station plugged into the wall with a router, modem or CPAP plugged into it, and the station takes over when the grid drops. No UPS function is a substitute for a dedicated UPS on equipment that cannot tolerate any interruption, so check your device's needs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Expansion for Longer Outages</h3>

          <p>
            The Expansion Battery 2000 lists capacity options from 3,072Wh to 22,528Wh when stacked with a Power 1000 or Power 2000. Expansion adds runtime, not output: the station's inverter still sets how much you can run at once.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="solar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Solar Charging a DJI Power Station: the Adapter Catch
          </h2>

          <p>
            DJI power stations are often sold as solar generators, but the listings for the Power 1000, 1000 V2 and 2000 all carry the same note: the MPPT and car charging modules are not built in.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the MPPT Module Does</h3>

          <p>
            An MPPT controller converts a solar panel's variable output into a charge the battery can accept efficiently. Most power stations from other brands have one inside. On these DJI models you connect panels through a separate DJI Power Solar Panel Adapter Module, which plugs into the station's SDC port.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Budget for It</h3>

          <p>
            When you compare a DJI with a rival, add the cost of the adapter and the panels. The original Power 1000's listing quotes up to 800W of solar input for a 1.35-hour recharge at maximum speed, which needs a large array in good sun.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Real Sun Delivers Less</h3>

          <p>
            Panels rarely produce their rated wattage. Angle, heat, cloud and time of day often cut output by a quarter or more, so plan for longer recharges than the listing's best case. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-charge-a-portable-power-station-with-solar/">charging a power station with solar</a> covers panel sizing and wiring.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drones" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Charging DJI Drone Batteries From a Power Station
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the one area where DJI's stations do something rivals do not. Every model lists fast charging for select DJI drone batteries through its SDC ports, using DJI's own cables.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How SDC Fast Charging Works</h3>

          <p>
            The SDC port connects straight to a DJI drone battery through a dedicated cable, skipping the drone's normal AC charger. The Power 1000 V2 listing says SDC Super Fast Charging supports takeoff in 30 minutes with select DJI drone batteries.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which Drones Are Covered</h3>

          <p>
            The Power 500 listing names Mavic 3 Series and Air 3 Series batteries. Compatibility varies by drone and cable, so check DJI's compatibility list for your model before buying cables.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Any Station Can Still Charge a Drone</h3>

          <p>
            You can charge a DJI drone from any power station by plugging the drone's normal charger into an AC outlet or a USB-C port. DJI's advantage is speed and fewer conversion losses, which matters for all-day shoots more than for occasional flying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="road-trips" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Car Charging and Road Trips
          </h2>

          <p>
            A power station in a car or van can top up while you drive, but on the DJI models covered here, that needs the right cable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the Car Combo Exists</h3>

          <p>
            Because the 1000-series and 2000 listings say car charging modules are not built in, DJI sells a Car Power Outlet to SDC Power Cable. The Power 1000 V2 Car Charging and Storage Bag Combo includes one rated for 12V and 24V, along with a padded storage bag.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Set Realistic Expectations</h3>

          <p>
            A car accessory socket supplies only a modest current, so a drive tops up a 1kWh battery slowly. Treat it as a way to replace what you used overnight on a long driving day, not as a quick refill. For faster charging on the road, a campground hookup or a wall outlet is still best.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep It Secure</h3>

          <p>
            Strap the station down so it cannot slide in a hard stop, and keep vents clear. A padded bag protects it from scuffs but should be opened while the station is charging or running so it can cool. Our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">best portable power stations for camping</a> guide covers other road-trip options.
          </p>
        </div>

