              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The Power 1000, 1000 V2 and 2000 listings say the MPPT module is not built in. Buyers who order panels alone find they cannot connect them. Add DJI's solar panel adapter module to the order.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Power 500 to Run Appliances</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Its 1,000W maximum output will not run a kettle, hair dryer or most microwaves. If you want appliances, start with a 1kWh model.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying V2 Prices for the Original</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The original Power 1000 is a good station, but the V2 has more output, faster charging and a quicker UPS. Only choose the original when it is clearly cheaper.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting Expansion to Add Output</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Expansion batteries add hours, not watts. If your loads exceed the station's inverter rating, a bigger battery will not help; you need a bigger station.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backfeeding a House Outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Never connect a power station to a wall outlet to power your home's wiring. Plug appliances in directly, or have an electrician install a transfer switch, and follow DJI's instructions and local codes.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best DJI power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the DJI Power 1000 V2 is the best DJI power station. Its listing gives 1,024Wh of LFP capacity, 2,600W of output, 0 to 80 percent charging in 37 minutes and a 10 ms UPS switchover. Choose the Power 2000 for expandable home backup, or the Power 500 if you mainly charge devices and drone batteries.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between the DJI Power 1000 and 1000 V2?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both hold 1,024Wh. The V2 is listed at 2,600W of output against 2,200W, recharges to 80 percent in 37 minutes against a full recharge in 70 for the original, and has a UPS switchover DJI calls twice as fast. The V2 listing also highlights dual 140W USB-C ports. Runtime for a given load is the same.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the DJI Power 2000 expandable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. The DJI Power 2000 listing says it supports expansion up to 22.5kWh with DJI expansion batteries, which are sold separately. Each Expansion Battery 2000 adds 2,048Wh, and up to ten can be stacked. Expansion adds runtime only; the Power 2000's own 3,000W inverter still sets how much you can run at once.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a DJI power station run a refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, the 1000-series and Power 2000 can. As a rough planning figure, a 120W-average fridge runs about seven hours on a 1kWh unit and about fourteen on the Power 2000, assuming 85 percent usable capacity. The Power 500 can run one only briefly. Check your fridge's actual draw; older models use much more.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do DJI power stations have built-in solar charging?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They charge from solar, but the Power 1000, 1000 V2 and 2000 listings say the MPPT and car charging modules are not built in. You need DJI's separate solar panel adapter module between the panels and the station's SDC port. Include that adapter in your budget when comparing DJI with other brands.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the DJI Power 1000 Mini sold on Amazon?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                We could not find a new Amazon US listing for the DJI Power 1000 Mini when we checked, only accessories for it. DJI's website lists it at 1,008Wh. If it becomes available, compare its output and ports with the Power 1000 V2, which has the same class of capacity and a listed 2,600W output.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do DJI power stations last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Power 500 and Power 1000 listings rate their LFP batteries for up to 4,000 cycles and about 10 years of service life, and the Power 1000 V2 listing also cites a 10-year lifespan. Most listings include a 5-year warranty. Storing the unit cool and partly charged helps the battery last.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
